Eosinophilic Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (Churg-Strauss)
Respiratory System
Refractory asthma, Nasal polyposis, p-ANCA/Eosinophilia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-018
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of treatment-resistant asthma symptoms accompanied by new-onset severe numbness and weakness in the right foot, requiring urgent multi-system evaluation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient with a history of refractory asthma now developing acute neurological deficits (foot drop) and abdominal pain. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to evaluate for acute systemic vasculitis (mononeuritis multiplex) and initiate high-dose systemic immunosuppression before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My asthma inhalers aren't working at all, my stomach is cramping, and since this morning I can't lift my right foot properly."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports adult-onset asthma that is entirely refractory to standard high-dose inhaled therapy. They have a known history of chronic rhinosinusitis with recurrent nasal polyposis. Over the past 48 hours, they have experienced severe shortness of breath, recurrent fevers, and unexplained weight loss. Today, they developed sharp gastrointestinal mucosal pain and a new, sudden inability to dorsiflex the right foot, presenting as mononeuritis multiplex.
Functional Status: Ambulatory with a severe limp; highly distressed by the new neurological symptom.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding the sudden loss of motor function.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/82 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 37.9 C | RR: 22 breaths/min | SpO2: 95% on room air | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Appears acutely unwell, tachypneic, and visibly limping when attempting to walk.
Physical Exam: Chest auscultation reveals widespread, tight expiratory wheeze bilaterally. Neurological exam confirms a right peroneal nerve palsy (foot drop) with decreased sensation over the dorsum of the foot, consistent with mononeuritis multiplex. Skin examination reveals scattered palpable purpura on the lower extremities. Cardiovascular exam shows tachycardia but no overt signs of heart failure. Previous records note p-ANCA positivity and peripheral blood eosinophilia (>10%).
Environmental Observations: No obvious environmental allergens.
Medication Reconciliation: Compliant with maximal asthma inhaler therapy with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Eosinophilic Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (Churg-Strauss) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Typical Allergic Asthma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete refractoriness to bronchodilators, presence of systemic vasculitic signs (purpura), and acute mononeuritis multiplex.

Microscopic Polyangiitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Strong history of adult-onset asthma and nasal polyposis, which are hallmark features of EGPA.

Diabetic Neuropathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Acute, asymmetrical onset of the nerve palsy (mononeuritis multiplex) rather than a chronic, symmetrical stocking-glove distribution.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe exacerbation of EGPA, evidenced by the classic triad of refractory asthma, nasal polyposis, and now acute systemic vasculitic manifestations (mononeuritis multiplex and palpable purpura). The sudden onset of right foot drop indicates active, organ-threatening vasculitis causing nerve ischemia. Delaying assessment and intervention until the next in-hours period would risk permanent neurological damage, further gastrointestinal ischemia, or progression to eosinophilic myocarditis. An urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to rapidly identify the systemic nature of the flare and immediately initiate high-dose systemic corticosteroids to halt the vasculitic process.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered high-dose oral Prednisolone 1mg/kg (70mg) immediately. Provided a stat dose of nebulized salbutamol (minimal effect noted, as expected). Facilitated urgent referral to Rheumatology/Immunology.
Positive Behavioral Change: Record your daily temperature and any new areas of numbness in a dedicated notebook every evening; maintaining this precise log provides critical data to the specialist, directly guiding the necessary adjustments to your immunosuppressive medications.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None at this stage.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ankle-foot orthosis (AFO) will be required for the foot drop.
Follow-up: Urgent specialist review within 48 hours is mandatory; patient advised to contact their treating Rheumatologist first thing in the morning.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate Emergency Department presentation (call 000) if severe abdominal pain develops (risk of bowel perforation), if chest pain or severe shortness of breath occurs (risk of myocarditis), or if weakness spreads to other limbs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The new nerve damage is a sign of systemic inflammation; the high-dose steroids are required immediately to prevent irreversible damage.
Clinical Course: The asthma symptoms should improve rapidly with high-dose steroids, but the recovery of the damaged nerve (foot drop) may take weeks to months and is not guaranteed.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go directly to the emergency room if you develop crushing chest pain, severe agonizing stomach cramps, or if you suddenly lose strength in your hands or other leg.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is aggressively attacking your blood vessels, which has choked off the blood supply to the nerve in your leg; the massive dose of steroids is required to immediately shut down this immune attack.
Actionable Steps: Do not stop or reduce the steroid medication under any circumstances until seen by the specialist; abruptly stopping this high dose will cause the blood vessel inflammation to rebound violently, risking severe organ damage.

